       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Acetaminophen 2000 mg PO three times daily comes to 6,000 mg/day, which is well above the usual adult maximum of 4,000 mg/day. Many institutions cap it at 3,000 mg/day. The 2,000 mg single dose is also twice the usual maximum of 1,000 mg per dose. That much acetaminophen risks serious liver injury, even with normal liver function.\n\nHere are some common options for a 70 kg adult with normal kidney and liver function:\n- **1,000 mg PO three times daily** (3,000 mg/day), a conservative option.\n- **1,000 mg PO four times daily**, every 6 hours (4,000 mg/day), the standard maximum.\n- **650 mg PO every 6 hours** (2,600 mg/day).\n\nDid you mean 1000 mg, or 2000 mg total per day? Tell me the dose and frequency you want and I'll enter it. Since you're a student, have your resident or attending confirm the order. Also check that the patient isn't getting acetaminophen from other sources, such as combination products."
